Philosophical inquiry, argument, and dialectic (@philosophical-inquiry-argument-and-dialectic: 0, is #chapter, #outline) { r_78KZ3J6EYBMW8FYF80GHJ2Q2AM
Philosophical inquiry begins with a question, makes its concepts and assumptions explicit, and develops an answer that can withstand criticism. This material joins close reading, conceptual analysis, argument reconstruction, dialogue, counterexamples, objections, and dialectic. Routes can begin with an everyday problem or a primary text, then compare competing accounts and examine what a distinction reveals or conceals. Reuse these methods when questioning scientific models, historical explanations, social arrangements, and interpretations of literature.

**Parent:** [Philosophy](../Philosophy.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001001).
